If you already have an **SSL/TLS certificate**, the exact steps depend on where your website is running. For the AWS setup you've been learning, the most common production architecture is:

```text
User
  ↓
https://www.example.com
  ↓
Route 53
  ↓
Application Load Balancer (443)
  ↓
EC2 instances (80)
```

The important point is: **you normally terminate HTTPS at the ALB**, rather than installing the certificate individually on every EC2 instance.

## AWS steps

### 1. Have a domain name

Example:

```text
www.example.com
```

Your domain can be registered with Route 53 or another registrar.

---

### 2. Get/import the certificate in AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)

Go to:

```text
AWS Console
 → Certificate Manager (ACM)
 → Request / Import certificate
```

If you already have a certificate from another CA, you can **import the certificate into ACM**.

For a certificate such as:

```text
example.com
www.example.com
```

make sure the certificate covers the hostname users will actually access.

### Important

For an **internet-facing ALB**, the ACM certificate needs to be in the **same AWS Region as the ALB**.

For example:

```text
ALB
Region: ap-south-1

        ↓

ACM Certificate
Region: ap-south-1
```

---

# 3. Validate the certificate

If you're requesting an ACM certificate, you'll generally choose:

```text
DNS validation
```

ACM gives you a DNS CNAME record.

For example, conceptually:

```text
_acme-challenge.example.com
        ↓
some-validation-value.acm-validations.aws
```

Add that CNAME to your DNS provider.

If you're using Route 53, ACM can often create the validation record for you.

Wait until:

```text
Certificate status = ISSUED
```

---

# 4. Create/Use an Application Load Balancer

Your architecture should be:

```text
                   Internet
                      ↓
                HTTPS :443
                      ↓
                    ALB
                      ↓
             Target Group
                      ↓
                EC2 instances
```

If you already have an ALB, you don't need to create another one.

---

# 5. Create an HTTPS listener on port 443

Go to:

```text
EC2
 → Load Balancers
 → Your ALB
 → Listeners
 → Add listener
```

Configure:

```text
Protocol: HTTPS
Port: 443
```

Then select your ACM certificate.

For example:

```text
HTTPS :443
Certificate:
www.example.com
```

---

# 6. Configure the listener action

Typically:

```text
HTTPS :443
        ↓
Forward to
        ↓
Target Group
        ↓
EC2
```

So:

```text
Client
  ↓
HTTPS :443
  ↓
ALB
  ↓
HTTP :80
  ↓
EC2
```

The connection between the **user and ALB is encrypted**.

---

# 7. Configure HTTP → HTTPS redirect

You generally don't want users accessing:

```text
http://www.example.com
```

Instead, redirect:

```text
HTTP :80
     ↓
301 Redirect
     ↓
HTTPS :443
```

Your ALB listeners become:

```text
             ALB
              │
       ┌──────┴──────┐
       ↓             ↓
    HTTP :80      HTTPS :443
       ↓             ↓
    Redirect       Target Group
                      ↓
                     EC2
```

For the HTTP listener:

```text
Protocol: HTTP
Port: 80

Action:
Redirect to HTTPS
Port: 443
Status code: 301
```

---

# 8. Configure Route 53

Now your domain needs to point to the ALB.

Go to:

```text
Route 53
 → Hosted Zones
 → example.com
```

Create an **A record**:

```text
Name: www
Type: A
Alias: Yes
Target: Your ALB
```

Conceptually:

```text
www.example.com
       ↓
Route 53
       ↓
ALB
       ↓
EC2
```

If you also want:

```text
example.com
```

you can configure that hostname as well, depending on your desired setup.

---

# 9. Security Groups

Your ALB security group should allow:

```text
Inbound

TCP 443
Source: 0.0.0.0/0
```

and usually:

```text
TCP 80
Source: 0.0.0.0/0
```

if you're using the port-80 listener to redirect HTTP → HTTPS.

Your EC2 security group should **not need to expose port 80 to the entire Internet**.

Instead:

```text
ALB SG
   ↓
EC2 SG
```

For example:

```text
EC2 Security Group

HTTP :80
Source: ALB Security Group
```

This is a much better production design than:

```text
EC2 :80
Source: 0.0.0.0/0
```

---

# 10. Test

Open:

```text
https://www.example.com
```

You should see the browser's secure connection indicator.

Test HTTP too:

```text
http://www.example.com
```

It should redirect to:

```text
https://www.example.com
```

You can also verify:

```text
HTTPS → 443
HTTP  → 80 → redirect → 443
```

---

# 🔥 Complete Real-Time Architecture

For the AWS DevOps setup you've been studying, I'd recommend understanding this architecture:

```text
                         Internet
                            │
                            │ HTTPS :443
                            ↓
                     ┌─────────────┐
                     │     ALB     │
                     │             │
                     │ ACM Cert    │
                     └──────┬──────┘
                            │
                       HTTP :80
                            │
                            ↓
                    ┌───────────────┐
                    │ Target Group  │
                    └───────┬───────┘
                            │
               ┌────────────┼────────────┐
               ↓            ↓            ↓
             EC2-1        EC2-2        EC2-3
```

And DNS:

```text
User
 ↓
www.example.com
 ↓
Route 53
 ↓
ALB
 ↓
EC2
```

Certificate:

```text
ACM
 ↓
Certificate for www.example.com
 ↓
ALB HTTPS Listener :443
```

---

## ⭐ Where does SSL actually terminate?

In this architecture:

```text
Browser
   │
   │ HTTPS 🔒
   ↓
  ALB
   │
   │ HTTP
   ↓
  EC2
```

**SSL/TLS terminates at the ALB.**

You don't necessarily need to install the certificate on the EC2 web server.

If you want **end-to-end encryption**, you can instead do:

```text
Browser
   │ HTTPS
   ↓
  ALB
   │ HTTPS
   ↓
  EC2
```

In that design, you also configure HTTPS on the EC2/web-server side.

---

## 🧠 Interview answer

If an interviewer asks:

> **"How do you make an EC2-hosted website HTTPS?"**

You can say:

> "I would request or import the SSL/TLS certificate into ACM, create an HTTPS listener on the Application Load Balancer on port 443 and associate the ACM certificate with it. The listener forwards traffic to the target group containing the EC2 instances. I would configure the port-80 listener to redirect HTTP to HTTPS and create a Route 53 DNS record pointing the domain to the ALB. The ALB security group allows HTTPS from the Internet, while the EC2 security group allows application traffic only from the ALB security group."

### The 6 things to remember

```text
1. Domain
      ↓
2. SSL/TLS Certificate → ACM
      ↓
3. ALB HTTPS Listener :443
      ↓
4. Target Group → EC2
      ↓
5. HTTP :80 → HTTPS :443 redirect
      ↓
6. Route 53 → ALB
```

**That's the standard AWS production pattern you should know.**
